 ## Overview
-The Ray Tracing Kernel is a CUDA compute kernel that performs forward and backward volumetric ray marching through a sparse hashed density grid, handling spherical harmonics (SH) evaluation and ray bounds [S2, unofficial]. It serves as the core inference and training step for the IDDR-NGP backend, computing final pixel colors and log-transmittance in the forward pass, and gradients for SH coefficients and grid outputs in the backward pass [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-
+The Ray Tracing Kernel is a CUDA compute kernel that performs forward and backward volumetric ray marching through a sparse hashed density grid, handling spherical harmonics (SH) evaluation and ray bounds [S2, unofficial]. It serves as the core inference and training step for the IDDR-NGP backend, computing final pixel colors and log-transmittance in the forward pass, and gradients for SH coefficients and grid outputs in the backward pass [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It differs from standard uniform ray marching by integrating SH evaluation directly into the kernel launch and from dense voxel cone tracing by operating on a sparse grid structure with warp-level synchronization [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
